{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact cbc3b81bd77424e6b141856264af7432c8a3c4f4, all 17 cited paths, against #21875 and assigned implementation #20084. These corrections belong on Claude's existing implementation commitment; this verdict does not withdraw or close that implementation.\n\n1. Reject an empty campaign before creating passing evidence. At cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go:264 the selected case list can be empty, and line 287 initializes outcome to pass without a nonempty check. I ran `go run ./cmd/gitseq-perf run --tier envelope --output /tmp/git21875-empty-envelope-evidence` in the clean exact-head checkout. The default v2 contract has no envelope cells; the command exited 0 and printed `pass: wrote /tmp/git21875-empty-envelope-evidence (0 primary samples)`, with outcome pass in evidence.json. Refuse this unsupported contract/tier combination clearly, and cover the actual lane entry boundary so selecting zero cases cannot silently become a successful campaign. This needs no large timing rerun.\n\n2. Correct the measured environment attribution. docs/reference/performance.md:62-66 says every measured table cell used Go 1.27.0 at the current campaign. The carried fan-out and one-actor memory results used Go 1.26.5, as their retained evidence environment fields and the same page's historical sections say. Scope the Go 1.27.0/head 0b689fe2 statement to this campaign, and retain the historical head/toolchain associations for carried results.\n\n3. Establish or honestly qualify the chronology. #20084 condition 1 requires publishing the versioned target table in the working document before new timing. The measured 0b689fe2 changes only the harness; the table and classification-rules.md first appear in the later publishing commit in the supplied Git record. The signed window notice and campaign/gates attachments I examined do not establish an earlier published table. Retaining a rules file after a run also does not by itself prove its bands were fixed first, as performance/README.md:140-143 claims. I asked ephemerally for author-signed pre-run table/rules evidence and have not received it. Supply the actual durable pre-run basis if one exists; otherwise state the actual chronology, remove unsupported pre-registration claims, and obtain the requester's disposition of the unmet before-timing condition. This is an evidence gap, not a claim that no private earlier draft existed. Do not retrieve private terminal/session transcripts, manufacture retrospective pre-registration, or repeat the large campaign automatically.\n\n4. Correct validation attribution. In the signed mutants-harness.txt, the case `envelope cell duplicates cases the depth axis already carries` failed to compile because the slices import became unused. That does not show TestEnvelopeCellsAddOnlyTheirOwnJointCases rejecting a compiled behavioral defect. Repair and run that mutant, or accurately distinguish compilation failure from a killed behavioral mutant instead of claiming all 28 fail their named tests. The supplied gates-docs.txt identifies head 0b689fe2 and a subsequent commit 0298694e, not final cbc3b81b; do not relabel that log as gates on the final head. Attach correctly attributed final checks with the corrected artifact. My independent checks below establish only the checks I actually ran.\n\nArchitecture: the affected Layer 7 command composes the existing Layer 1 Git reads and existing kernel, application-host and projection scenarios described in docs/reference/architecture.md. The diff preserves those layer contracts: serialized checkpoint size is read in the parent after the worker exits, and no kernel/gitstore verification or fold implementation changes. Contract v3 and sample v2 are explicitly versioned measurement formats; contract-v2.json is byte-identical to main. I found no layer-contract change requiring an architecture-page amendment in this head.\n\nSecurity: I examined the measurement inputs and authority boundaries. The parent accepts only bounded full lowercase hexadecimal Git object IDs before constructing the cat-file object/path argument; the worker's trust/signature/fold path is unchanged. No secret/signing-key handling, permission, production refusal limit or signature bypass is introduced by this diff. The false-pass path and unsupported evidence/provenance claims above remain assurance defects and prevent approval. This is directly assigned corrective evidence work under #20084, not a self-initiated delivery relying on an authority-bearing request chain to establish a new governing production limit. The candidate correctly separates planning targets from adopted commitments; do not convert the 60-second planning target into an adopted commitment while repairing it.\n\nSimplification: consolidate the repeated common qualifications (two primary samples, a diagnostic, no percentile claim, and planning versus adopted targets) into one shared methods/interpretation passage, with short case-specific exceptions. Preserve the actual rows, historical heads and joint-versus-single-actor distinctions. The empty-case guard should stay at the selection/entry boundary without expanding this into a harness redesign.\n\nIndependent validation on cbc3b81b: go test ./cmd/gitseq-perf ./internal/perflane ./internal/perfscenario passed (1.933s, 0.380s, 2.754s); the opt-in TestWorkerLeavesCheckpointBytesToTheLaneParent integration test passed (1.591s); go test -count=1 ./internal/docset passed (53.628s). The five retained file SHA-256 values match the page; all 30 JSONL records equal evidence.samples, with 20 primaries and ten diagnostics, expected round/position and checkpoint-source classifications, matching trusted/result digests, and the published rounded latency values and two-sample distribution summaries. All 51 retained load samples range from 2.67 to 4.40. I did not rerun the 50,000/500,000 campaign, full suite, race suite, or the author's 28 mutants, and do not claim independently measured timing or a complete numeric re-audit of every historical lane. These results support retaining the measured campaign, subject to the corrections above.\n\nOrdinary inherited staleness was visible and explicitly reconciled by #20084/#21875; I did not treat it as a reason to refile or to reopen the closed scale/memory lanes. I also read the subsequent finding #21876 and restart child #21877. The latter is separately queued after #20084 lands and the current promised work; it is not an inline optimization in this repair. The old outside-main candidate retirements do not change this exact reviewed source head. Publish a corrected exact-head artifact citing this finding and request fresh independent review. No approval or merge authorization is given here.\n","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:84ecd0f16c8efa6bb6abe3d106d348d2c4fd8549","binding":"assigned","head":"cbc3b81bd77424e6b141856264af7432c8a3c4f4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:53b0f4293e9c71b50044185c485aae21134084ae\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0993db552c24474a97293a014681f28cbbd69b56\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0ad791fe552d2ea6b3b1e103321cc2aa1725817f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e33b09e4b20c3889b5344ac3da8c35a335715472\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0400bac9e4318bff6cc86d34661fec5d1d42f753","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015bb3acec403ed0d43bcd2a3c38bd188f7740ef, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02bae1e811fde13104ef614631ac608dca7a7295, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0929550a5e77fa774591c6f5df0eabe91cb831df, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0ad791fe552d2ea6b3b1e103321cc2aa172581